# MoE OTD async batched miss-uploads (staging ring, non-blocking copies)
#
# fill_weights_memory (src/graph/impls/ocl_v2/moe/moe_otd_runtime.cpp) read
# each missed expert's tensor from disk into a loop-local std::vector<uint8_t>
# payload, transposed it in place, and copy_from(..., blocking=true)'d it to
# the device slot - one blocking GPU copy per (expert, tensor) pair, and
# OffloadExpertWeightProvider::try_acquire_simultaneous
# (expert_weight_providers.cpp) called it once per individual miss inside its
# loop rather than once for the whole batch. This patch batches the misses of
# one try_acquire_simultaneous call into one fill_weights_memory call (that
# function already took experts_list/lru_experts as vectors; the batching is
# in the caller, not a new code path), and inside it replaces the per-tensor
# blocking copy with a non-blocking one out of a provider-owned usm_host
# staging ring, waiting on the whole batch's events once. Applied against the
# pinned runtime, build 2026.4.0-22849-71640275d29, on top of patches/0003-
# 0005; rebased onto 0003 rather than reintroducing the per-infer expert-mask
# subbuffer churn that patch removed (moe_3gemm_swiglu_opt.cpp's scratch_
# buffers / on_before_batched_gemv / on_before_prefill region, which 0003 and
# this patch both touch, do not conflict: 0003 caches expert-mask subbuffers,
# this patch only changes what happens to a *miss* inside try_acquire_
# simultaneous).
#
# Payload lifetime, not the blocking flag, is what a naive change here would
# get wrong: payload is freed at the end of each loop iteration, and a non-
# blocking enqueue_memcpy started from it could still be reading when the
# caller returns - use-after-free. StagingRing (moe_otd_runtime.hpp/.cpp) is
# the fix: a small ring of usm_host buffers, provider-owned (one per
# OffloadExpertWeightProvider, so MoE layers do not contend on one ring and
# usm_host allocations amortize across infers rather than being rebuilt per
# call). StagingRing takes no lock: it is single-stream-only by assumption
# (see the comment at the class), true today because it is one-per-provider
# and each provider is only ever driven from its own network's exec stream.
# acquire() waits on a slot's previous copy before handing it back for
# reuse (the only wait cost this patch can impose independent of the queue-
# type gate below); maybe_transpose_scale_zp still writes into the existing
# loop-local payload vector (kept as-is, not touched, to keep this diff to
# the upload path) and a plain host-to-host memcpy stages it into the ring
# slot, which is what the non-blocking device copy then reads from.
#
# Where the wait happens: a single wait over the whole batch's collected
# events, inside fill_weights_memory, right before it returns - which is
# also right before try_acquire_simultaneous's caller (on_before_batched_gemv
# / on_before_prefill / exec_prefill_grouped_gemm,
# moe_3gemm_swiglu_opt.cpp:727-800/840-914) calls set_otd_weight_pointers,
# the last point before the impl enqueues the GEMMs (docs/design-m9-offload-
# v2.md §1, P2's wait-placement bullet). Gated on
# stream.get_queue_type() == QueueTypes::in_order (execution_config.cpp:344
# forces in_order whenever use_onednn is set, true for both cards this ran
# on); on an out_of_order queue fill_weights_memory falls back to the
# pre-patch synchronous per-tensor path unchanged, rather than trying to
# prove out-of-order correctness here. On the in_order queue this wait is not
# what makes the GEMM kernels read correct data - program order already does
# that once the copy is enqueued before them - it exists solely to make the
# ring's slots safe to reuse on the next miss batch.
#
# StagingRing's slot count (8, a placeholder) is not a correctness parameter;
# docs/design-m9-offload-v2.md §4 names it [to measure]. New counter
# (patches/0004): staging_bytes - current ring footprint in bytes, expected
# to plateau after warm-up per the design's own P2 validation row
# ("staging_bytes bounded and constant across infers").
#
# gpu_copy_ns quietly changes meaning on the async path: it used to be one
# blocking copy_from's wall time, summed per tensor; now, when async_ok, it
# is the single batch-end wait's wall time instead (the individual enqueues
# are not timed - they are non-blocking, so there is nothing to time at the
# enqueue site), still summed across batches. Same counter name, same units,
# a different thing being measured; avg_gpu_copy_us derived from it in
# dump() divides by tensor_load_count as before, which now reads as an
# average amortized over a whole batch's wait rather than a true per-tensor
# cost. Left as one counter rather than split in two, since the two
# situations do not coexist in one build's steady state (a build either
# takes the async path throughout or does not) and splitting would grow
# OtdPerfCounters for a distinction the async/sync gate already makes.
#
# Measurement discipline: not run on a GPU (this task's rules); the byte-
# exact-vs-P1-only-build and stage-acc-median checks the design requires of
# this patch (docs/design-m9-offload-v2.md §4's P2 row) are owed to the
# orchestrator's on-GPU pass, not claimed here.
#
# REGRESSION, measured on-card after this patch and 0007 shipped together:
# u8-KV serving produces deterministic garbage under the patched plugin
# (vanilla plugin coherent, same everything else; reproduces at pool cap 0,
# so patches/0005 is not implicated); u4-KV through the same patched plugin
# is coherent. The expert-upload path this patch changes is identical across
# KV precisions, so the leading theory is a timing-exposed race: u8 and u4
# PagedAttention kernels run at different speeds, and that shifts which
# interleaving of async work this patch's wait sees. This invalidates the
# cycle-2 sweep numbers taken under this patch until resolved (fast wrong
# compute) - flagged on the campaign record, not just here.
#
# Kill-switch for on-card isolation: MOE_OTD_FORCE_SYNC_UPLOAD=1 forces
# async_ok false unconditionally in fill_weights_memory, routing every call
# (both try_acquire_simultaneous's batch and acquire_one's single-expert
# path, since both funnel through this one function) down the pre-0006
# synchronous per-tensor branch that already exists for out_of_order queues
# - i.e. this build behaves as if only patches/0003-0005 were applied to the
# upload path. Read once, the same way MOE_OTD_PERF_LOG is read; default
# off, unchanged from patches/0006 as landed. Paired with 0007's
# MOE_OTD_FORCE_FINISH=1 (independent switch, same env-var pattern), the two
# can be toggled in the 2x2 the regression grid needs: sync+finish (should
# match pre-0006/0007 behaviour, the control), sync+no-finish, async+finish,
# async+no-finish (patches/0006+0007 as landed, the arm that is currently
# suspect).
#
# Re-audit, with the race lens: this patch's own correctness argument (wait
# inside fill_weights_memory before it returns, ahead of set_otd_weight_
# pointers) still looks sound on paper - the wait is on the real completion
# event of each enqueued USM copy (traced through ocl_memory.cpp's
# create_event/gpu_usm::copy_from: a fresh cl::Event per call, no pooling or
# reuse that could let a wait be satisfied by the wrong command), and every
# copy plus the GEMV kernel that reads its result go through the one
# cl::CommandQueue a cldnn::stream owns (ocl_stream.hpp/ocl_common.hpp:
# ocl_queue_type is cl::CommandQueue, one per stream, no separate copy vs
# compute queue) - so in-order execution should serialize them regardless of
# blocking vs non-blocking submission. What this argument does NOT cover,
# and where confidence has to drop given the measurement: it reasons about
# THIS call's own events, not about whether the *previous* iteration's GEMV
# kernel — which reads the same slot buffer this call's copy is about to
# overwrite — is provably ordered before this call's enqueues in every
# calling context, including whatever host-side interleaving arcint's
# serving loop actually does across tokens (single-token decode was assumed
# strictly sequential host-side; that assumption was not verified against
# the live serving loop, only against the code in this file). The batch-
# internal slot-reuse case (two misses in one call landing on the same LRU
# slot) was checked and is capacity-bounded safe. Net: nothing here is
# retracted, because nothing here is independently confirmed broken - but
# the confidence this header stated before the measurement was too high,
# and the actual fault most likely sits in 0007's argument instead (see that
# patch's header) or in an interaction between the two that neither header
# analyzed on its own.
